This paper studies how hedge fund activism reshapes corporate boards beyond disclosed activist appointments. Using a comprehensive sample of activist campaigns from 1994 to 2024, we estimate “activist-induced turnover”: board changes that would not have occurred absent an activist campaign. We show that activism’s effect on board composition is substantially larger than appointment-based measures suggest, and that campaigns strengthen the link between poor firm performance and director replacement.
